why 5 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices move down with the bass's descending scale. They fall by different intervals, so each line keeps its own shape.
why 8 0 SEVENTH The chord over G♯ is really the dominant, E major, with the soprano's C♯ passing. The tenor's B is its fifth, not a seventh, and is free to leap to F♯.
why 8 0 OVERLAP The tenor leaps up to F♯, above where the alto was, while the alto leaps up to A. They move up together.
why 10 0 DOUBLING In this cadential ⁶₄ soprano and tenor share C♯: the soprano falls C♯–B and the tenor climbs C♯–D–E into V. The doubling follows their lines.
why 11 1/2 LEADING_TONE The bass's G♯ falls to F♯ for a deceptive turn to F♯ minor (vi). The A that G♯ leads to is already in the alto and tenor.
why 12 0 LEADING_TONE The music is turning to E major, where G♯ is the third of the goal chord, not a leading tone. The alto steps down G♯–F♯ toward the half cadence on E.
why 13 0 DOUBLING Bach darkens the harmony with G and B♭, a diminished chord. Alto and bass share G; it is not a leading tone, so the doubling is safe.
why 13 1/2 LEADING_TONE The tune leaps up from C♯ to F♯, and Bach keeps the hymn melody. The D that C♯ leads to is in the bass.
why 13 1/2 DOUBLING The tune leaps up to F♯ while the alto steps G–F♯; both land on the third of the D chord, with the root D in the bass.
